  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improving Maternal and Child Health Outcomes</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Laos):  .The Laos Maternal Child health and Nutrition (LMCHN) activity will address inequitable health and nutrition outcomes of the most underserved segments of the population through a targeted health system strengthening approach. It will demonstrate innovative models, building on lessons learned from previous USG and other international development partners&apos; investments to improve health and nutrition outcomes. LMCHN will strengthen equitable access to quality health and nutrition services for adolescents, specifically the most vulnerable girls (10 to 19 years old) to ensure young pregnant women can access quality services...LMCHN will support the Government of Laos to have greater ability to steward, design, manage, and monitor a system of high-quality, integrated multi-sectoral approach reproductive, maternal, newborn, child, and adolescent health and nutrition services. Communities will strengthen their roles as active agents of change in service quality, and access to, and use of services, and the adoption of behaviors to improve health outcomes. LMCHN&apos;s goal is to improve health and nutrition outcomes among pregnant and postpartum women, newborns, children, and adolescents. LMCHN contributes to the Mission&apos;s Health Strategy 2019-2023 Intermediate Results 2.1, 2.2, and 2.4, in support of its &quot;Laos has improved health and education systems that foster a healthier and more productive population&quot; Development Objective. ..USAID/Laos has identified this activity for an external impact evaluation, in accordance with the USAID Evaluation Policy. This evaluation will allow USAID to rigorously test the effectiveness of LMCHN, the findings of which will greatly inform future USAID, other donor, and Government of Laos health efforts..This IM has previously received Nutrition funds; however, the current substantial pipeline means Laos will not use FY 2022 Nutrition funds for this IM....</narrative>
  </description>
